{"@context":["https://litlfred.github.io/folio-assistant/ns/content/v1.jsonld",{"@base":"https://litlfred.github.io/folio/","folio-assistant-core":"https://litlfred.github.io/folio-assistant-core/v0/ns/"}],"@id":"papers/quantum-observable-universe/blocks/tbl-tm-notation-table3-data","@type":["folio-assistant-core:Table","doco:Table"],"label":"tbl:tm-notation-table3-data","kind":"table","title":"Data table 3 from tm-notation","uses":["papers/quantum-observable-universe/blocks/tm-notation"],"tags":["extracted-table"],"text":"tm-notation-table3-data.md","provenance":"authored","html":"<a id=\"tbl:tm-notation-table3-data\"></a><p>This table assigns each lepton its primary knot or link closure (with the\nsingle-component trefoil alternative noted for <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">e^{\\mp}</code>) and lists the\nmirror closure carried by its antiparticle. A quark is an unknot strand\nof the Borromean braid <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\mathcal{B}_3</code>\n(<a href=\"#def:up-quark\" data-at=\"knots-particles-confinement/nucleon-derivation\">up quark</a>); its rows record the <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">(2,n)</code> torus-knot\ngeneration label of its lepton partner\n(<a href=\"#rem:knot-particle-dictionary\" data-at=\"knots-particles-confinement/skein-particle-generation\">knot--particle dictionary</a>), not\nthe quark's own knot type.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Particle</th>\n<th>Knot / link</th>\n<th>Antiparticle</th>\n<th>Knot / link</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Neutrino <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\nu</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">T_{1,0}</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\nu^{\\sigma}</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">T_{1,0}^{\\sigma} = T_{1,0}</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Electron <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">e^-</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">T(2,2)</code> (<code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\sigma_0\\sigma_1\\sigma_0 \\in B_3</code>; alt. <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">T_{2,3}</code>)</td>\n<td>Positron <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">e^+</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">T(2,2)^{\\sigma}</code> (alt. <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">T_{2,3}^{\\sigma}</code>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Up quark <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\mathfrak{u}</code> (partner)</td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">T_{2,3}</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\mathfrak{u}^{\\sigma}</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">T_{2,3}^{\\sigma}</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Down quark <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\mathfrak{d}</code> (partner)</td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">T_{2,3}^{\\sigma}</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\mathfrak{d}^{\\sigma}</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">T_{2,3}</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Charm quark <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\mathfrak{c}</code> (partner)</td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">T_{2,5}</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\mathfrak{c}^{\\sigma}</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">T_{2,5}^{\\sigma}</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Strange quark <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\mathfrak{s}</code> (partner)</td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">T_{2,5}^{\\sigma}</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\mathfrak{s}^{\\sigma}</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">T_{2,5}</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Bottom quark <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\mathfrak{b}</code> (partner)</td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">T_{2,7}^{\\sigma}</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\mathfrak{b}^{\\sigma}</code></td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">T_{2,7}</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">T_{3,4}</code> (Hopf<code class=\"language-math math-inline\">{}^2</code> binding)</td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">T_{3,4}</code></td>\n<td>(self-conjugate in <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">H^{0,1}</code>)</td>\n<td><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">T_{3,4}^{\\sigma}</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Here the parameters <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">(p,q)</code> are the standard torus-link indices, and the\nsuperscript <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\sigma</code> denotes the particle–antiparticle (bar)\nconjugation: the antiparticle carries the knot <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">T_{p,q}^{\\sigma}</code>,\nand a knot satisfying <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">T_{p,q}^{\\sigma} = T_{p,q}</code> (such as the\nneutrino's <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">T_{1,0}</code>) is self-conjugate. The grading <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">H^{0,1}</code> is\nwhere this self-conjugacy is registered.</p>\n","source":"folio/quantum-observable-universe/appendix-mass-binding-algorithms/tm-notation-table3-data.md","heading":"Table","page":"b/tbl-tm-notation-table3-data","qa":{"counts":{"pass":41,"fail":0,"warn":0,"na":17,"unknown":0,"stale":57}}}
